-- Procreate Tutorial for Absolute Beginners
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['State what hardware Procreate actually requires and what it costs here, and name the free alternatives if that hardware is out of reach', 'Set up a canvas correctly the first time, knowing that its size fixes your maximum layer count and cannot be changed afterwards', 'Use layers, alpha lock, clipping masks and selections well enough to keep a drawing editable instead of flattened'],
  notes = 'Before anything else, the honest part. Procreate runs on iPad ONLY. Not Android, not Windows, not iPhone for the full app. It is a one-off purchase from the App Store in dollars, which is cheap compared with a subscription, but the app is the small cost.

WHAT IT REALLY COSTS TO START

- An iPad that supports the current version. A used one is the realistic route for most people here; check which models the current Procreate version still supports before buying, because older iPads get dropped.
- An Apple Pencil, or a supported stylus. This is not optional. Drawing with a finger is possible and miserable, and the Pencil''s pressure sensitivity is what makes line weight work at all.
- A card that works for App Store purchases.

Add those up in shillings before you commit to this path. For many learners the sum is a few months of income, and it is better to know that now than after three lessons.

IF THAT HARDWARE IS OUT OF REACH, YOU HAVE NOT LOST ANYTHING

Everything about drawing transfers between apps. Only the button locations change. Real alternatives, all of which teach the same skills:

- KRITA, free and open source, on Windows, Mac and Linux. A full painting application with no cost at all. If you have any laptop, this plus a cheap drawing tablet is the cheapest serious setup there is.
- ANDROID TABLETS AND PHONES with Infinite Painter, ibisPaint X, MediBang Paint or Autodesk Sketchbook. Free or very cheap, and genuinely capable for illustration and stickers.
- ADOBE FRESCO, which is the next lesson, has a free tier and runs on more devices.

Do not wait for the perfect setup. The person who draws every day on a KSh 12,000 Android tablet will be better in six months than the person saving for an iPad.

THE ONE SETTING YOU CANNOT UNDO

When you create a canvas you choose its pixel dimensions and its DPI. Learn this now, because it catches nearly everybody:

YOU CANNOT CHANGE A CANVAS SIZE LATER WITHOUT LOSING QUALITY, AND THE SIZE DECIDES HOW MANY LAYERS YOU GET.

Procreate allocates layers based on canvas size and your iPad''s memory. A huge canvas may give you only a handful of layers; a modest one gives you dozens. Beginners pick the largest canvas available, discover at layer six that they cannot add another, and start again.

So decide what the drawing is FOR before you create it:

- FOR SCREENS ONLY - social posts, a phone wallpaper, a digital sticker: 72 DPI is enough, and the pixel dimensions are what matter.
- FOR PRINT - a sticker, a t-shirt, a poster: 300 DPI at the real physical size. A sticker 5 cm wide at 300 DPI is about 590 pixels wide. Work it out before you start, because scaling a 72 DPI drawing up for print produces a blurry mess and there is no fix.
- WHEN UNSURE, go slightly larger than you need at 300 DPI. Scaling DOWN loses nothing. Scaling UP loses everything.

THE FIVE THINGS THAT MAKE THE APP USABLE

LAYERS. Separate parts of the drawing onto separate layers: sketch, line, flat colour, shading, background. The whole reason is that you can change one without redrawing the others. A drawing on one layer is a photograph of a decision you can no longer revise.

ALPHA LOCK. Restricts painting to the pixels already on that layer, so you can shade inside a shape without going outside it. Use it on a filled shape when you want to add texture to that shape only.

CLIPPING MASKS. A layer that only shows where the layer beneath it has pixels. Same effect as alpha lock, but non-destructive and on its own layer, so you can delete or adjust it freely. Prefer this when you are still experimenting.

LAYER MASKS. Hide parts of a layer without erasing them. White reveals, black conceals. This is how you remove something and still get it back.

SELECTIONS AND TRANSFORM. Select a region, then move, scale or rotate it. Note the interpolation setting when scaling; for pixel-crisp work it matters.

GESTURES WORTH LEARNING ON DAY ONE

Two fingers to undo, three to redo, pinch to zoom, pinch quickly to fit the screen, two-finger tap and hold for the eyedropper, and swipe down with three fingers for the cut-copy-paste menu. Learning these five is the difference between fighting the app and forgetting it is there.

Also: the brush, smudge and eraser all use the SAME brush library. An eraser with a soft airbrush behaves completely differently from one with a hard pencil, and beginners who do not know this think their eraser is broken.

COLOUR, AND THE DROP THRESHOLD

You can drag a colour from the corner swatch into an enclosed shape to fill it. The threshold - how far the fill spreads - is set by how far you drag before releasing. If your fill leaks across the whole canvas, your outline has a gap in it. That is almost always the cause.

Keep a palette rather than picking colours at random each time. Five or six colours that work together will make a drawing look deliberate.

THE FEATURE THAT IS ALSO A BUSINESS TOOL

Procreate records a time-lapse of everything you draw, automatically. Most people treat this as a toy for social media. It is also evidence: when a commission client or a marketplace questions whether a piece is your original work, the time-lapse settles it. Export and keep the time-lapse for any paid work. Lesson 5 returns to this.

EXPORTING WITHOUT RUINING IT

- Keep the layered Procreate file as your master. Always.
- PNG for anything needing a transparent background - stickers, logos, art to place on a product.
- JPEG only for flat photographs of finished work where transparency does not matter, because it loses quality every time it is saved.
- Flatten only on export, never in the working file.

And back the master files up somewhere that is not just the iPad. A lost or stolen tablet with a year of unbacked-up work in it is a story told far too often.',
  practice_activity = 'Set up correctly, then make one small drawing that stays editable the whole way through.

Step 1. Before touching the app, write down your situation honestly:

- Device you will actually use: ____
- Stylus: ____
- If you do not have an iPad and Pencil, which alternative from the notes you will use instead: ____
- Total cost in shillings of the setup you are choosing: ____

Step 2. Decide what your drawing is FOR, then calculate the canvas. Write all four lines:

- Purpose: screen or print
- Final physical size if print: ____ cm
- DPI: ____
- Pixel dimensions: ____ by ____

Step 3. Create that canvas and immediately write down how many layers the app gives you. Then create a second canvas at four times the dimensions and write down its layer limit. Keep both numbers. That comparison is the lesson.

Step 4. Draw something simple - a mug, a plant, a face - on at least four separate layers: sketch, line, flat colour, shading. Name every layer.

Step 5. Use each of these once, and write one line on what it did:

- Alpha lock, to add texture inside one shape
- A clipping mask, to shade without touching the colour layer
- A layer mask, to hide part of something and then bring it back
- A selection and transform, to move one element

Step 6. Break the colour fill on purpose. Leave a gap in an outline, drop a colour in, and watch it flood the canvas. Then close the gap and do it again. Now you will recognise that failure instantly for the rest of your life.

Step 7. Export three files from the same drawing: the layered master, a PNG with a transparent background, and a JPEG. Write down the three file sizes and which one you would send to a print shop.

Step 8. Export the time-lapse and save it with the master. Write one line on why you would keep this for paid work.

Step 9. Copy the master file somewhere that is not the tablet. Write where.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "You create the largest canvas your device offers and run out of layers at layer six. What happened?", "options": ["The app is faulty and needs reinstalling", "Procreate allocates layers based on canvas size and device memory, so a very large canvas yields few layers - and canvas size cannot be changed afterwards", "You need to buy a layer upgrade", "Layers are limited to six on all canvases"], "correct_index": 1}, {"question": "You drew a design at 72 DPI for Instagram, and now a client wants it printed as a 10 cm sticker. What is the honest position?", "options": ["Scale it up to 300 DPI in the app and print it", "Print it anyway, since stickers are small", "Scaling up cannot add detail that was never captured, so it must be redrawn at the print size and 300 DPI", "Change the DPI setting in the export dialogue and the quality will follow"], "correct_index": 2}]'::jsonb
where id = 'afdf6ab2-6c3b-469c-b82f-7c7e2bd0d90c';

-- Adobe Fresco Tutorial for Beginners
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Explain the difference between raster and vector in one sentence each, and choose the right one for stickers, logos and paintings', 'Use live brushes and the raster-plus-vector document as the thing Fresco does that other apps do not', 'Work out what the free tier covers, where the cloud storage limit bites, and what a subscription would cost you monthly in shillings'],
  notes = 'Fresco is Adobe''s drawing and painting app. Two things make it worth knowing even if you already have another app: it has a free tier, and it holds raster and vector artwork in the SAME document. That second one is the concept this lesson is really about.

RASTER AND VECTOR, THE DISTINCTION THAT DECIDES EVERYTHING

RASTER artwork is a grid of pixels. A photograph is raster. A painting with soft blended edges is raster. It has a fixed resolution: there are only so many pixels, and enlarging it beyond that invents nothing, it just makes the existing pixels bigger and softer.

VECTOR artwork is a set of mathematical paths - points, curves, a stroke width, a fill. It has no resolution. The same vector logo can be printed on a business card or on the side of a matatu with identical crispness, because the shape is recalculated at whatever size you ask for.

Which to use:

- A PAINTING, with texture, blending and soft edges: raster. Vector cannot do a convincing watercolour.
- A LOGO: vector, always. A client will one day need it enormous, and a raster logo cannot deliver that. Lesson 5 of the motion graphics course meets the same problem from the other side.
- A STICKER OR ICON with clean flat shapes: vector is usually better, because you can scale it to any print size later.
- LINE ART you might need at several sizes: vector.

Fresco lets you have both in one file, which is unusual and genuinely useful: paint a textured background on raster layers and put crisp vector line art on top.

LIVE BRUSHES, THE OTHER THING THAT IS DIFFERENT

Fresco''s live brushes - watercolour and oil - simulate wet media. The paint spreads into the surrounding area, colours bleed into each other, and oil paint has thickness that picks up the colour beneath it as you drag through it.

Two practical consequences:

- They keep reacting for a moment after you lift the stylus. Let a watercolour stroke settle before judging it.
- They are heavier on the device than ordinary brushes. On an older tablet, work on a smaller canvas when using them.

They are the most enjoyable part of the app and the least transferable. The layer and colour skills below are what you take everywhere.

WHAT THE FREE TIER ACTUALLY GIVES YOU

Adobe''s plans change, so check the current terms on their own page rather than trusting any tutorial, including this one. The shape of it has been:

- A free tier with a substantial set of brushes, the core raster and vector tools, and a limited amount of cloud storage.
- A paid tier adding the full brush library, more storage, and some Photoshop interoperability.

Two things to check for yourself before building a habit on it:

- THE CLOUD STORAGE LIMIT. Fresco leans on cloud documents. A few large illustrations will fill a small free allowance, and then you are choosing what to delete. Find the free limit, and find out whether you can keep documents locally instead.
- WHAT A SUBSCRIPTION COSTS PER MONTH IN SHILLINGS, including the exchange rate and your card''s cross-border fee. A dollar subscription is a permanent monthly cost, and over a year it can exceed the one-off cost of a different app entirely.

Fresco runs on iPad, on iPhone and on Windows, which makes it reachable on more hardware than an iPad-only app. If you have a Windows laptop and a cheap pen tablet, that is a working setup.

COLOUR, PROPERLY

Two things worth getting right early, because they apply in every app:

RGB versus CMYK. Screens mix red, green and blue light. Printers mix cyan, magenta, yellow and black ink. Some bright screen colours - vivid oranges, electric blues, pure greens - simply cannot be reproduced in ink, and will come back from the printer duller than you expected. If a piece is destined for print, test-print one before you commit to a hundred. Lesson 4 returns to this.

A LIMITED PALETTE. Pick five or six colours and stay with them. Choose one dominant colour, one accent that contrasts with it, and a few neutrals. Random colour picking is the commonest reason a competent drawing still looks amateur.

LAYERS AND MASKS, SAME IDEAS, DIFFERENT BUTTONS

Everything from the previous lesson applies: separate layers for sketch, line, colour and shading; clipping masks to shade inside a shape; masks to hide rather than erase. Fresco adds the wrinkle that a layer is either raster or vector and you cannot convert one into the other by wishing - decide before you draw.

WORKING BETWEEN APPS

You will end up moving work around. Keep two habits:

- Export a PNG with transparency for anything that will sit on a product, a post or a video.
- Keep the layered master in the app that made it, and keep a copy off the device.

And one honest note for a Kenyan context: cloud-first apps sync constantly, which costs data. If you are on a bundle, check whether the app will let you work offline and sync later, and watch your usage in the first week rather than discovering it on the bill.',
  practice_activity = 'Prove to yourself that you understand raster against vector, then price the app honestly.

Step 1. Make one small drawing TWICE in the same document: once on a raster layer and once on a vector layer. A simple leaf or a star is enough.

Step 2. Now scale both up to four times their size. Look closely at the edges. Write one sentence describing exactly what you see on each. This single test is the whole lesson and most people never run it.

Step 3. Decide and write the right choice for each of these, with a reason:

- A logo for a tailoring business: ____
- A watercolour portrait: ____
- A sticker that will be printed at 5 cm and also used at 512 pixels for WhatsApp: ____
- A textured background for a book cover: ____

Step 4. Use a live brush. Paint one watercolour stroke, wait for it to settle, then paint a second colour into it and watch them bleed. Write one line on how the result differs from an ordinary brush.

Step 5. Build a limited palette of six colours: one dominant, one accent, four neutrals. Use only those in a small finished piece. Write down why you chose the accent.

Step 6. Do the money arithmetic from the vendor''s own pages, not from this lesson:

- Free tier cloud storage: ____
- Size of one of your finished documents: ____
- Number of documents before the free tier is full: ____
- Monthly subscription cost in shillings, including exchange rate and card fee: ____
- Annual cost: ____

Then write one sentence on whether that is the right spend for you, or whether a one-off purchase or a free application serves you better.

Step 7. Test the print-colour problem cheaply. Pick the most vivid colour you can make on screen, export it, and if you can, have a print shop print a small swatch. Compare them side by side and write what changed.

Step 8. Check your data. Note your data usage before and after an hour of work with cloud sync on. Write the figure. If it is high, find the offline setting.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "A client will need their logo on a business card and later on a three-metre banner. What format must the artwork be?", "options": ["A high-resolution raster PNG, which covers both", "Vector, because paths are recalculated at any size with no loss, while raster has a fixed pixel count", "Either, as long as it is exported at 300 DPI", "JPEG at maximum quality"], "correct_index": 1}, {"question": "Your vivid orange design comes back from the printer looking dull. What is the explanation?", "options": ["The printer used the wrong file", "The export was at too low a DPI", "Screens mix light in RGB and printers mix ink in CMYK, and some bright screen colours cannot be reproduced in ink - which is why you test-print before committing", "The artwork was vector instead of raster"], "correct_index": 2}]'::jsonb
where id = '5e438ece-0430-4808-ae94-80741636112c';

-- A Quick and Simple Way to Draw a Cute Character
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Build a character from simple shapes rather than from outlines, and say why construction beats detail at the start', 'Use line weight, silhouette and proportion deliberately, since those three do most of the work in a drawing that reads well', 'Tell the difference between using reference, copying and infringing, and practise in a way that actually improves you'],
  notes = 'The video shows a method. These notes name the principles underneath it, because the principles are what let you draw the next character without the video.

CONSTRUCTION FIRST, DETAIL LAST

Beginners draw the outline of a face and then try to fit features into it. That is backwards and it is why the result feels wrong without anybody being able to say why.

Build instead:

1. A simple shape for the head - circle, oval, rounded square. The shape choice already carries personality: round reads friendly, square reads sturdy, triangular reads sharp.
2. A centre line and an eye line across it. These two lines decide where the face is looking and are the single biggest source of beginner errors.
3. Simple shapes for the body, arranged as a pose, with no detail at all.
4. ONLY NOW the features, placed on those guides.
5. Detail and texture at the very end, if at all.

Keep the construction on its own layer, at low opacity, under everything. That is what those layers in the first lesson were for.

PROPORTION, AND WHY CUTE IS A FORMULA

Cuteness is not vague. It is a consistent set of proportions, and knowing them makes it repeatable:

- A LARGE HEAD relative to the body. An adult figure is roughly seven to eight heads tall. A cute character is two to four.
- EYES LOW ON THE FACE and large. Placing eyes below the horizontal midline of the head is the single strongest cue.
- SMALL NOSE AND MOUTH, SHORT LIMBS, ROUNDED EVERYTHING. Soft shapes read as harmless.

Change one of those deliberately and the character changes. Narrow the eyes and raise them and the same character becomes shrewd rather than sweet.

SILHOUETTE: THE TEST THAT CATCHES PROBLEMS EARLY

Fill your drawing entirely black and look at the shape. Can you still tell what it is? Can you tell what the pose is doing?

If the black shape is unreadable, the drawing has a structural problem that no amount of shading will fix. Professionals check this constantly; beginners never do. It takes five seconds with a layer filled in black behind everything, or by turning your line layer into a single filled shape.

LINE WEIGHT: FREE DEPTH

Lines of one uniform thickness look flat and mechanical. Vary them with intention:

- THICKER on the outer contour of the character, thinner for interior detail. This separates the figure from the background instantly.
- THICKER where a form is in shadow or where two shapes overlap, thinner where light falls.
- THICKER for the large forms, thinner for small ones.

This is what pressure sensitivity on a stylus is for. If your lines are all the same width, you are drawing with the pressure curve switched off, or pressing uniformly hard.

EXPRESSION IS THREE PARTS, AND MOSTLY THE BROWS

An expression lives in the relationship between brows, eyes and mouth - and the brows carry most of it. A smiling mouth with lowered brows is not happy, it is smug. A neutral mouth with raised brows is surprised.

The useful exercise is to draw the same face six times changing ONLY the brows, then six more changing only the mouth. You will learn more from those twelve drawings than from six finished illustrations.

REFERENCE, COPYING AND INFRINGEMENT: THREE DIFFERENT THINGS

This matters and it is usually skipped.

USING REFERENCE is looking at how a real hand folds, how cloth hangs, how a chicken''s leg joints work, and applying it in your own drawing. Every professional does this constantly. Drawing from memory alone is how people develop the same wrong hand for ten years.

COPYING, as practice, is reproducing somebody else''s drawing to learn how they did it. Legitimate, useful, and PRIVATE. Do not post it as your own work, and do not sell it.

INFRINGEMENT is selling or commercially using somebody else''s character or artwork. Drawing a well-known cartoon character and selling it on a t-shirt is infringement, however much you changed the pose. Platforms remove this and rights holders do pursue it. Lesson 6 covers the business side.

The practical rule: learn from everybody, trace nobody for publication, and sell only what you made up.

HOW TO PRACTISE SO IT ACTUALLY WORKS

Deliberate practice beats volume. Two habits:

- DRAW THE SAME THING TEN TIMES, not ten different things once. Ten versions of the same character in different poses teaches you the character''s construction. Ten different characters teaches you nothing you can repeat.
- CHANGE ONE VARIABLE AT A TIME. Same character, six expressions. Same pose, three different line weights. Same face, three head shapes.

And keep your old drawings with dates. Improvement at this is invisible day to day and obvious across three months, and the folder is the only thing that will convince you on the days when it feels like nothing is working.',
  practice_activity = 'Draw one character properly, then prove the principles on it.

Step 1. Pick your character in one sentence: what it is and one personality word. "A sleepy mandazi seller" is better than "a cute girl".

Step 2. On a construction layer at low opacity, lay down only: a head shape, a centre line, an eye line, and simple body shapes in a pose. No features, no detail. Stop and look at it. If the pose is unclear now, it will be unclear finished.

Step 3. Write your proportion choices before drawing features:

- Heads tall: ____
- Eye line above or below the head''s midline: ____
- Head shape and the personality it carries: ____

Step 4. Add features on the guides. Then detail. Keep the construction layer underneath, hidden but not deleted.

Step 5. Run the silhouette test. Fill the whole character solid black and look at it. Write down whether it still reads, and what you changed if it did not.

Step 6. Fix the line weight deliberately. Go over the outer contour thicker and the interior detail thinner. Compare before and after side by side and write one sentence on the difference.

Step 7. Do the expression drill. Draw your character''s face six times changing ONLY the brows. Then six more changing only the mouth. Write which of the twelve surprised you.

Step 8. Do the repetition drill properly. Draw the SAME character in ten different poses. Not ten characters. Note which pose was hardest and why.

Step 9. Use real reference for one thing you guessed at - a hand, a shoe, the way a kanga folds. Find a photograph, look at it, and redraw that part. Write what you had been getting wrong.

Step 10. Write three lines you can state plainly: one thing you learned by copying privately, one thing you learned from reference, and one character you will never sell because it is not yours.

Date the page and keep every drawing.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Your finished character looks wrong but you cannot say why. Which check is most likely to find the problem?", "options": ["Adding more shading and texture", "Filling the whole figure solid black and checking whether the silhouette still reads", "Increasing the canvas resolution", "Changing the colour palette"], "correct_index": 1}, {"question": "You want to practise and improve. Which is the better use of an afternoon?", "options": ["Draw ten completely different characters once each", "Draw the same character in ten poses, changing one variable at a time", "Trace ten professional drawings and post them", "Watch ten tutorials without drawing"], "correct_index": 1}]'::jsonb
where id = '0afbe148-6fd7-493c-ba47-c18ec092aa30';

-- How to Draw Stickers in Procreate
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Prepare a sticker file that a print shop can actually use: correct size, 300 DPI, transparent background, a die-cut outline and bleed', 'Plan a physical sticker order around minimum quantities, material and unit cost, and never skip the single test print', 'Produce a WhatsApp sticker pack to the platform''s own requirements, as a free distribution route'],
  notes = 'Stickers are the best first product for an illustrator: small, cheap to produce, easy to sell, and forgiving of a simple drawing style. The drawing is the easy half. The file preparation and the ordering are where money is lost.

SET THE CANVAS UP FOR THE REAL WORLD

Work backwards from the physical object, exactly as the first lesson insisted:

- DECIDE THE FINISHED SIZE IN CENTIMETRES first. A 5 cm sticker and a 10 cm sticker are different drawings; detail that reads at 10 cm disappears at 5.
- 300 DPI, not 72. A 5 cm sticker at 300 DPI is roughly 590 pixels across.
- ADD BLEED. Make the canvas slightly larger than the finished size - a few millimetres on each side - and let your artwork run into that margin. Cutting machines are not perfectly accurate, and without bleed a slight misalignment leaves a thin white edge on one side.
- KEEP IMPORTANT DETAIL AWAY FROM THE EDGE, inside a safe margin, for the same reason.

THE DIE-CUT OUTLINE, WHICH BEGINNERS FORGET ENTIRELY

A cut sticker needs a line telling the machine where to cut. Usually that is a white or coloured border offset outwards from your artwork - the familiar sticker look. It exists for a practical reason as well as a decorative one: it gives the cutter somewhere to be slightly wrong.

Build it as a shape slightly larger than your drawing, on its own layer behind everything, with a smooth outline. Avoid thin spikes and narrow necks - a cutter cannot follow them, and a sticker with a 1 mm neck tears when peeled.

Ask your printer what they want: some accept a visible border and cut around it, some want a separate cut-path layer or file. Ask before you design, not after.

TRANSPARENT BACKGROUND, AND THE RIGHT FILE

- PNG with transparency for anything that must not have a white box around it.
- 300 DPI, at the physical size you specified.
- Keep the layered master separately. Always.
- Check the transparency actually survived: open the exported PNG and look for a white rectangle. A flattened background is the commonest file error a print shop receives.

If the printer asks for PDF, export at the same specification and check the colour mode they want.

COLOUR WILL SHIFT, AND THAT IS NORMAL

As the previous lesson said: screens are RGB light, printers are CMYK ink. Vivid oranges, bright greens and electric blues come back duller. Neon is not achievable in ordinary printing.

So: choose colours slightly away from the extremes if a piece is destined for print, ask the printer whether they want RGB or CMYK files, and accept that the result will not match your screen exactly. Which leads to the rule that saves the most money.

ORDER ONE TEST PRINT. ALWAYS. BEFORE THE HUNDRED

One sheet, printed on the actual material, by the actual shop, before you place the real order. Look at it in daylight. Check the colour, the cut accuracy, how it peels, whether the fine lines survived, whether it is readable at the real size.

A test costs a little and catches everything: the washed-out colour, the detail that vanished, the outline that cut through your artwork. People skip it to save a few hundred shillings and lose thousands.

THE ORDERING ARITHMETIC

Before you promise anybody a price, find out from a local print shop:

- MINIMUM ORDER QUANTITY. Many will not print fewer than a sheet or a fixed count. This decides your up-front cost.
- MATERIAL. Vinyl is durable, waterproof and costs more; paper is cheap and will not survive a water bottle. For stickers people stick on laptops and phones, vinyl is what they expect.
- LAMINATION, matte or gloss, which adds cost and protects the print.
- COST PER SHEET, and how many of your stickers fit on a sheet. Fitting more designs per sheet is how the unit cost falls.
- TURNAROUND TIME, which you need before promising a delivery date.

Then: unit cost plus your time plus delivery, against the price you can actually charge. If a sticker costs you KSh 25 to produce and people here will pay KSh 50, your margin is KSh 25 and you need volume. Know that before ordering 500.

WHATSAPP STICKER PACKS: A FREE CHANNEL

This is the distribution route most illustrators here overlook, and it costs nothing.

WhatsApp sticker packs have their own specification, and you must check the current one on the platform''s own documentation rather than trusting a tutorial. Historically the requirements have been close to: 512 by 512 pixel PNG files with transparency, a small file-size limit per sticker, a minimum and maximum number of stickers per pack, plus a separate small tray icon.

Why bother: a pack that people actually use spreads by itself, every single day, with your style attached to it. It is the cheapest marketing an illustrator has. Make the pack about something people here genuinely say to each other, keep the drawings readable at thumbnail size, and put your name on the pack rather than on every sticker.

Note that readability at 512 pixels on a phone is a real design constraint. Fine lines and small text disappear. Bold shapes and strong contrast survive.

AND THE RULE FROM THE LAST LESSON, RESTATED

Sell only what you invented. A sticker of a famous cartoon character is infringement no matter how well drawn, and it is the fastest way to have a shop or a platform remove your work and your account.',
  practice_activity = 'Make one sticker to print specification, and one WhatsApp pack.

Step 1. Decide the physical object before drawing:

- Finished size: ____ cm
- DPI: 300
- Canvas pixel dimensions including bleed: ____ by ____
- Bleed added on each side: ____ mm

Step 2. Draw the sticker, keeping all important detail inside a safe margin. Then add the die-cut outline on its own layer behind the artwork - offset outwards, smooth, with no thin necks or spikes.

Step 3. Export a 300 DPI PNG with transparency, then OPEN THE EXPORT and confirm there is no white box. Write what you checked.

Step 4. Talk to a real print shop, in person or by message, and write down their answers:

- Minimum order quantity: ____
- Material options and prices: vinyl ____ paper ____
- Lamination cost: ____
- Cost per sheet, and how many of your stickers fit per sheet: ____
- Unit cost: ____
- Turnaround: ____
- What file format and colour mode they want, and whether they need a separate cut path: ____

Step 5. Do the margin arithmetic:

- Your unit cost: KSh ____
- The price people here will actually pay: KSh ____
- Margin per sticker: KSh ____
- Stickers you must sell to cover the minimum order: ____

Step 6. Order ONE test print. Write down what you found when you held it: colour against your screen, cut accuracy, how it peels, whether fine detail survived. Then write the one change you are making before the real order.

Step 7. Build a WhatsApp pack. Look up the CURRENT requirements on the platform''s own documentation and write them down - dimensions, file type, transparency, size limit, number of stickers, tray icon. Then make a pack of drawings about things people around you actually say.

Step 8. Test the pack on a phone. Send a few to a friend and look at them at real size in a chat. Write down which ones failed because the lines were too fine, and fix them.

Step 9. Write one line confirming every design in both the print run and the pack is your own invention.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Why does a printed sticker file need bleed and a die-cut outline?", "options": ["To make the colours brighter", "Because cutting machines are not perfectly accurate, so artwork must run past the cut line and the cutter needs a path with no thin necks", "Because PNG files require a border", "To reduce the file size"], "correct_index": 1}, {"question": "You are about to order 500 vinyl stickers of a new design. What is the step people skip and regret?", "options": ["Converting the file to JPEG", "Increasing the canvas to the largest possible size", "Ordering one test print on the actual material from the actual shop first, and checking colour, cut and fine detail in daylight", "Adding a watermark to the artwork"], "correct_index": 2}]'::jsonb
where id = 'f766d969-50ce-4208-8ff0-b26b91c28a89';

-- 6 Steps to Selling Your First Art Commissions
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Run a commission through a process that protects both sides: written brief, deposit, sketch approval, limited revisions, final files on full payment', 'Price by what the client may DO with the art rather than by hours, and say what rights each price includes', 'Get paid reliably from Kenya, and know what to keep as evidence if a client disputes the work'],
  notes = 'A commission goes wrong in predictable ways: the brief was vague, no deposit was taken, the client asked for a seventh revision, or the finished file was sent before the money arrived. A process fixes all four. This lesson is that process.

STEP 1: A WRITTEN BRIEF, BEFORE ANY DRAWING

Never start from a spoken description. Write it down and have the client agree to it, even if that is a WhatsApp message they reply yes to.

The brief must contain:

- WHAT the piece is: a portrait, a logo, a book cover, two characters.
- THE SUBJECT in specifics, with reference images the client supplies - photographs of the people, the pet, the product.
- STYLE, agreed by pointing at examples of YOUR existing work, not somebody else''s. "Like this piece of mine" is enforceable; "modern and fun" is not.
- SIZE AND USE: screen only, or printed, and at what size. This decides your canvas, as lesson 1 explained.
- DEADLINE, and what happens if the client is slow to respond - because they will be, and your deadline should pause when they go quiet.
- WHAT IS NOT INCLUDED. The most useful line in any brief.

STEP 2: PRICE THE USE, NOT THE HOURS

Quoting hours invites an argument about your hourly rate and punishes you for getting faster. Price the piece and the RIGHTS.

The same drawing is worth very different amounts depending on what the client may do with it:

- PERSONAL USE. A portrait for somebody''s wall or profile picture. Cheapest.
- COMMERCIAL USE. The client will use it to sell something - product packaging, a logo, a shop sign, merchandise. Substantially more, because it earns them money repeatedly.
- EXCLUSIVE OR FULL TRANSFER. The client owns it outright and you cannot reuse or display it. Most expensive, because you are giving up an asset and a portfolio piece.

State which one your price includes, in writing. The commonest expensive mistake a beginner makes is charging a personal-use price for a logo that then appears on a business''s signage, packaging and vehicles.

Also decide and state: do you keep the right to show it in your portfolio? Usually yes, and say so. For confidential work, a client may pay more for you not to.

STEP 3: A DEPOSIT, BEFORE YOU DRAW

Half up front, and say plainly that it is non-refundable once work begins. This is not rudeness; it is the single rule that removes almost every bad client, because people who never intended to pay do not pay deposits.

No deposit, no drawing. Hold that line even for people you know - especially for people you know.

STEP 4: SKETCH APPROVAL, THEN LIMITED REVISIONS

Work in stages and get agreement at each one:

1. A rough sketch - composition and pose only, no detail. Send it. Get written approval.
2. Line art or base colour. Approval again.
3. Finish.

Changes at the sketch stage cost minutes. The same change after you have rendered for six hours costs the six hours. The approval gate exists to force the client''s second thoughts to happen early.

Then state the revision limit in the brief: two rounds of minor changes included, further changes charged at a stated rate. "Minor" means adjusting a colour or a detail, not changing the pose or the subject - and say that too, because the word is otherwise elastic.

STEP 5: WATERMARKED PREVIEWS, FINAL FILES ON FULL PAYMENT

Everything you send before the balance is paid goes out at low resolution with a visible watermark across the artwork, not tucked in a corner where it can be cropped off.

The final files - full resolution, no watermark - go after the money has arrived and cleared. Not when the client says they have sent it. When you can see it.

And decide what the final files ARE. A flat PNG or JPEG is normal. The LAYERED SOURCE FILE is a separate thing of real value, and handing it over means the client can alter your work and reuse elements forever. If they want the source, charge for it and say so in the brief.

STEP 6: GETTING PAID, FROM HERE

- LOCAL CLIENTS: M-Pesa or bank transfer, in shillings. Simple, fast, and the money is real on arrival.
- INTERNATIONAL CLIENTS: PayPal, Payoneer or Wise, in dollars. Check the receiving fee, the currency conversion and the withdrawal threshold BEFORE quoting, and build them into the price. A 20 dollar commission can lose a meaningful share to fees.
- Note that card and PayPal payments can be reversed by the payer weeks later. For a large first-time international commission, a milestone structure protects you better than a single payment at the end.
- Keep a record of every payment: date, client, amount, what for, fees deducted. You need it for the next paragraph and for your own sanity.

Your commission income is income. Confirm your own position with KRA or an accountant rather than assuming small amounts are invisible; the thresholds for the different regimes change.

THE EVIDENCE THAT SETTLES DISPUTES

Two habits, both cheap:

- KEEP THE TIME-LAPSE that Procreate records automatically, as lesson 1 noted. If a client or a marketplace claims the work is not yours, a recording of you drawing it from a blank canvas ends the argument immediately.
- KEEP THE WRITTEN TRAIL: the brief, the approvals, the revision requests. A commission argument is nearly always about what was agreed, and the person with the messages wins.

WHERE THE FIRST CLIENTS COME FROM

The same place as in every other trade: people who already know you. Family businesses needing a logo, a friend wanting a portrait as a gift, a church or school needing an illustration, a small shop needing a sign.

Post your work publicly and consistently so that people who hear about you can see it. Freelance marketplaces exist and do produce work, but you are competing globally on price with a commission taken off the top, so treat them as one channel rather than the plan.

And price your very first commission low if you must, but ONLY once, deliberately, with the brief and deposit rules still fully in force. The process is not the part you discount.',
  practice_activity = 'Build the commission system before you need it, then run one piece through it.

Step 1. Write your brief template, with these headings filled in as blanks: what the piece is, subject and reference supplied by client, style shown by pointing at YOUR work, size and intended use, deadline and what pauses it, revision rounds included, what is NOT included.

Step 2. Write your price list with three tiers and a real figure in shillings for each:

- Personal use: KSh ____
- Commercial use: KSh ____
- Exclusive or full transfer: KSh ____
- Layered source file, if requested: KSh ____
- Portfolio rights: do you keep them? ____

Step 3. Write your deposit sentence, word for word, as you will actually send it. Then write the sentence you will use when somebody you know asks you to start without one.

Step 4. Write your revision clause. Define "minor" explicitly and state the charge for anything beyond the included rounds.

Step 5. Set up your preview watermark. Make one now, across the middle of the image, not in a corner. Export a watermarked low-resolution preview of an existing piece and check it cannot simply be cropped.

Step 6. Work out your international payment reality, from the providers'' own pages:

- Receiving fee: ____
- Currency conversion loss: ____
- Withdrawal threshold and fee: ____
- Net on a 50 dollar commission, in shillings: ____

Write one sentence on what you will add to your price to cover that.

Step 7. Run a real commission through all six steps, even a free one for a family business. At each stage write the date: brief agreed, deposit received, sketch approved, line art approved, final payment received, files delivered.

Step 8. Keep the evidence. Save the time-lapse and the full message trail in a folder named after the client. Write where that folder is.

Step 9. Start the income record: date, client, amount, fees, net, what for. Add one line naming what you will confirm with KRA about declaring this income.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "A client commissions a drawing at your personal-use price and it later appears on their product packaging and shop sign. What went wrong?", "options": ["Nothing - once paid, the client can use the art however they like", "The price did not state which rights it included, and commercial use should have been priced and agreed separately in writing", "The file resolution was too high", "The client should have asked for the layered source file"], "correct_index": 1}, {"question": "When should the full-resolution, unwatermarked final files be sent?", "options": ["At the sketch stage, so the client can see progress clearly", "When the client confirms they have sent payment", "After the balance has actually arrived and cleared", "Along with the layered source file, as a courtesy"], "correct_index": 2}]'::jsonb
where id = '66f93e8e-7595-4f0d-b5df-dbf5ebba9c75';

-- How to Turn Your Art Into Products
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Compare print on demand against local production on the three things that decide it from Kenya: who holds stock, who gets paid in what currency, and who pays delivery', 'Work out the real margin on one product, including unit cost, printing, delivery and payment fees, before producing anything', 'Sell only art you own, and recognise the three ways illustrators lose accounts over intellectual property'],
  notes = 'Putting your art on products is the obvious next step and the arithmetic decides whether it is worth doing. There are two routes and they are not equally suited to selling from Kenya.

ROUTE ONE: PRINT ON DEMAND

You upload artwork to a platform. A customer orders. The platform prints, packs, ships and pays you a cut.

- NO STOCK, NO UP-FRONT COST, no risk of unsold boxes. That is the genuine attraction.
- YOU EARN A SMALL MARGIN per item, set largely by the platform''s base cost.
- YOU GET NO CUSTOMERS. The platform''s own traffic sells a trickle to nobody in particular; meaningful sales come from an audience you bring.
- PAYMENT IS IN DOLLARS, through PayPal or a similar service, with a payout threshold. Same arithmetic as every other dollar-paying platform in this catalogue: check the threshold and the fees before building a plan on it.
- AND THE PART THAT MATTERS MOST HERE: shipping to and within Kenya is slow and expensive, and often costs more than the product. A Kenyan customer will not pay KSh 2,500 shipping on a KSh 1,500 t-shirt.

So the honest conclusion: print on demand suits an illustrator selling to an INTERNATIONAL audience they already have. It is a poor fit for selling to people around you.

ROUTE TWO: LOCAL PRODUCTION

You take your file to a printer in Nairobi, Mombasa, Kisumu or your own town. They print t-shirts, mugs, tote bags, notebooks, stickers. You hold the stock and sell it yourself.

- YOU SELL IN SHILLINGS and get paid by M-Pesa. No threshold, no conversion, money the same day.
- DELIVERY IS CHEAP AND FAST - a boda, a matatu parcel, or hand delivery.
- YOUR MARGIN IS MUCH LARGER, because nobody takes a platform cut.
- YOU CARRY THE RISK. You pay up front for a minimum quantity and you own whatever does not sell.
- YOU DO THE WORK: packing, delivery, answering messages, chasing payment.

For most illustrators selling to a Kenyan audience, this route earns more. The constraint is the up-front cost and the unsold-stock risk, and the way to manage that is to start with the cheapest product at the smallest minimum - usually stickers, as the previous lesson covered - and only move up to t-shirts once a design has proved it sells.

THE MARGIN CALCULATION, DONE PROPERLY

Do this for ONE product before producing anything. People skip it and discover they have been working for nothing.

- Unit cost from the printer, at the minimum quantity: KSh ____
- Your delivery cost per item: KSh ____
- Payment fee, if a payment service is involved: KSh ____
- Packaging: KSh ____
- TOTAL COST PER ITEM: KSh ____
- Selling price people will actually pay: KSh ____
- MARGIN PER ITEM: KSh ____
- Items you must sell just to recover the minimum order: ____

That last number is the honest one. If you must sell 60 t-shirts to break even and your audience is 200 people, write down how plausible that is before you pay the printer.

Also price by what the market bears, not by multiplying your cost. If similar t-shirts sell for KSh 1,200 at the market near you, KSh 2,500 will not move however nice your drawing is.

FILE PREPARATION, PER PRODUCT

Each product type has its own requirements, and the printer has a template. Ask for it.

General rules that hold everywhere:

- 300 DPI at the real printed size, as in every lesson in this course.
- Transparent PNG for anything printed onto a coloured garment, and TEST your design against the actual garment colour - a design with black line art disappears on a black t-shirt.
- Keep detail away from seams, hems and mug handles. Ask the printer where the unprintable zones are.
- Colours will shift to CMYK. Test print one.
- And one that catches people: a design that looks right on a screen may be the wrong proportion for the print area. Use the printer''s template rather than guessing.

Then the same rule as the sticker lesson, because it is the rule that saves the most money: ORDER ONE BEFORE YOU ORDER FIFTY. One t-shirt, washed twice, tells you whether the print survives. A print that cracks after one wash will cost you every customer you had.

THE THREE WAYS ILLUSTRATORS LOSE ACCOUNTS OVER RIGHTS

This section is short and it matters more than anything above.

1. FAN ART OF SOMEBODY ELSE''S CHARACTER. Drawing a famous cartoon or film character and putting it on a product is infringement. It does not matter that you drew it yourself, and it does not matter that everybody at the market does it. Platforms remove the listing and often the account; rights holders do act.

2. SOMEBODY ELSE''S PHOTOGRAPH OR ARTWORK AS A BASE. Tracing a photographer''s image, or building on art you found online, is infringement even if you changed the colours. If you did not take it or make it, you do not have the right to sell it.

3. IMAGES YOU CANNOT PROVE YOU OWN. If you used an AI image generator, read its terms on commercial use and on whether outputs can be exclusively yours - and understand that you may not be able to prove originality if challenged. For the same reason, keep your own process recordings, as lesson 5 said. Being able to show how a piece was made is worth more than any argument about it.

The rule is simple: sell only what you invented, from your own hand and your own references.

THE EXPECTATION TO SET HONESTLY

Product income without an audience is close to zero. This is the same truth stated in the affiliate marketing and the course-creation lessons elsewhere in this catalogue, and it is not pessimism, it is the arithmetic.

What works is the order: build a body of work and a small audience that likes your style FIRST, then put that style on products those specific people will buy. Uploading fifty designs to a platform and waiting is the version that earns nothing, and it is the version most people try.

And your product income is income. Keep the records - date, product, units, revenue, costs - and confirm your position with KRA rather than assuming.',
  practice_activity = 'Decide the route with arithmetic, then produce one real product.

Step 1. Answer in writing: is your audience mainly here or mainly abroad? Count honestly - the people who would actually buy, not followers. Then write which route that points to.

Step 2. Get real numbers from a local printer for ONE product you would actually sell:

- Product: ____
- Minimum order quantity: ____
- Unit cost at that quantity: KSh ____
- Their file template dimensions and DPI: ____
- Unprintable zones: ____
- Turnaround: ____

Step 3. Do the full margin calculation:

- Unit cost: KSh ____
- Delivery per item: KSh ____
- Payment fee: KSh ____
- Packaging: KSh ____
- Total cost per item: KSh ____
- Price the market will pay, checked against what similar items sell for near you: KSh ____
- Margin per item: KSh ____
- Items to break even on the minimum order: ____

Step 4. Write one sentence on whether that break-even number is plausible for your audience. If it is not, pick a cheaper product with a smaller minimum and redo the arithmetic.

Step 5. Prepare the file on the printer''s template: 300 DPI at printed size, transparent background, detail away from seams. Then check it against the actual garment or mug colour and write what you changed.

Step 6. Order ONE. Then test it properly: wash a garment twice, use a mug for a week. Write down what happened to the print.

Step 7. Run the ownership check on every design you plan to sell. For each, write one line: what it is, that you invented it, and what reference you used. Remove anything that fails.

Step 8. Price and list it where your audience actually is - an Instagram or WhatsApp catalogue, a market stall, a shop that will stock it. Write the three places.

Step 9. Start the product record: date, product, units produced, unit cost, units sold, revenue, costs. Add one line naming what you will confirm with KRA about this income.

Step 10. Write the honest expectation in one sentence: how many you think you will sell in the first month, and what you will conclude if you sell none.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Your audience is almost entirely in Kenya. Why is print on demand usually the wrong route for them?", "options": ["Print-on-demand quality is always poor", "Shipping to and within Kenya is slow and often costs more than the product, and payouts arrive in dollars against a threshold", "Kenyan customers cannot use the platforms", "Print on demand does not allow original artwork"], "correct_index": 1}, {"question": "You drew a well-known cartoon character yourself, in your own style, and want to sell it on t-shirts. What is the position?", "options": ["It is fine, because you drew it by hand rather than copying a file", "It is fine as long as you change the colours", "It is infringement regardless of your having drawn it, and platforms remove the listing and often the account", "It is fine for local sales but not for international ones"], "correct_index": 2}]'::jsonb
where id = 'b76c1342-07dd-412e-9d3f-17cc84ce40d6';
